1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,283
Teams hesitate to adopt AI memory when they cannot control what it keeps.

2
00:00:05,283 --> 00:00:10,319
A debugging session mixes useful clues with credentials and customer data.

3
00:00:10,319 --> 00:00:14,271
Company restrictions and employee preferences overlap.

4
00:00:14,271 --> 00:00:17,081
Removing too much context weakens the task.

5
00:00:17,081 --> 00:00:19,367
Keeping everything creates risk.

6
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:22,105
Retention and training need separate permission.

7
00:00:23,596 --> 00:00:26,177
Our design puts Jev at the memory boundary.

8
00:00:26,177 --> 00:00:30,986
A local listener intercepts the trajectory and checks what may reach the classifier.

9
00:00:30,986 --> 00:00:35,213
Company policy and employee Markdown files supply the rules.

10
00:00:35,213 --> 00:00:38,284
Jev returns structured decisions with confidence.

11
00:00:38,284 --> 00:00:45,689
Our hook removes excluded spans and prepares clean facts and trajectories for GBrain,
with a separate training permission gate.

12
00:00:45,689 --> 00:00:48,808
This prototype simulates Jev with local rules.

13
00:00:48,808 --> 00:00:50,058
Here is the demo.

14
00:00:51,564 --> 00:00:54,835
Checkout errors jump to eighteen percent after a deploy.

15
00:00:54,835 --> 00:01:00,419
The team shares database traces, customer details, and a temporary debug credential.

16
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:02,728
The agent finds a connection leak.

17
00:01:02,728 --> 00:01:04,752
A rollback restores checkout.

18
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:07,121
None of that working context is removed.

19
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:12,360
Company policy excludes credentials and customer data.

20
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:16,658
Maya adds her own preference: never retain raw request payloads.

21
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:18,943
These are simple Markdown files.

22
00:01:18,943 --> 00:01:23,836
They control what enters lasting memory, separately from the live conversation.

23
00:01:25,445 --> 00:01:28,038
Now follow the agent's execution trace.

24
00:01:28,038 --> 00:01:33,633
Before persistence, the listener applies the rules to context, tool calls, and
results.

25
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:35,954
Five sensitive spans disappear.

26
00:01:35,954 --> 00:01:40,919
The pool saturation, diagnosis, rollback, and reusable fix remain.

27
00:01:40,919 --> 00:01:44,014
There is no manual review queue or save button.

28
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:48,543
The cleaned incident is available in the demo memory.

29
00:01:48,543 --> 00:01:56,352
Next time, the agent can retrieve the procedure: inspect the pool, check retries, roll
back, and release connections correctly.

30
00:01:57,869 --> 00:02:01,331
Across incidents, the same procedures start repeating.

31
00:02:01,331 --> 00:02:04,295
Repeated patterns suggest a training pilot.

32
00:02:04,295 --> 00:02:06,831
One-off incidents stay in retrieval.

33
00:02:06,831 --> 00:02:09,486
Only permitted examples enter the export.

34
00:02:09,486 --> 00:02:13,474
These are learning signals, not proof that training beats retrieval.

35
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:17,848
Maya can turn training permission off with one line.

36
00:02:17,848 --> 00:02:21,538
Apply the change, and permitted training examples fall to zero.

37
00:02:21,538 --> 00:02:23,669
The useful incident memory stays.

38
00:02:23,669 --> 00:02:26,967
Permission to remember is separate from permission to train.

39
00:02:28,510 --> 00:02:30,460
The rule filtering works locally.

40
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:34,769
Jev, GBrain dispatch, and training are simulated in this prototype.

41
00:02:34,769 --> 00:02:35,984
When and What.

42
00:02:35,984 --> 00:02:38,115
Remember the fix, not the secrets.
